# ============================================================
#  Mini-plots (2026-10-25): short storylines of four to six cards, at the places whose visits most often came up
#  empty (grim report --gaps: the infirmary, the lecture tower, the home town). Each is a @thread from the story
#  template (docs/grimoire-script.md §9): its start card begins it, and every other card waits on that without saying
#  so. Plot-safe: none of them touches the main mystery.
#    missed_round  — the night Sister Amaranth missed her round (infirmary)
#    red_ink       — someone marks your essays in red ink (lecture tower)
#    late_bloomer  — the sweet shop's daughter and her late magic (home, across two breaks)
#  Reworked after retrieval (build/batches/miniplots_2026_10.notes.md): each premise was searched and each card checked
#  with `retrieve related --draft` against the existing cards it must not repeat.
# ============================================================

# ---------------- The missed round ----------------

@thread missed_round
from: story
with: begun=flag(rounds_begun)
class: story
repeat: 1
tags: story
clues: lead_missed_round
members:
  start infirmary_missed_round

@event infirmary_missed_round
title: The Lamp That Went Out
place: infirmary
time: evening
tone: tense
when: met(amaranth) and term and turn >= 6 and not flag(rounds_begun)
repeat: 3
weight: 14
art: lantern
scene: A dim infirmary ward at evening, a fever charm over the far bed glowing a dull red, a healer's desk lamp unlit.
text: The fever charm over the far bed has burned down to a red glow. It should have been turned an hour ago. Sister Amaranth does every round herself, and has never once missed one. Her lamp is dark.
> left: Go and find her
  health -tiny
  flag rounds_begun
  find lead_missed_round
  queue infirmary_sister_fevered now
  say: The evening tray is still on the ward table, every bottle full. Her office door, at the back, is open a crack, and dark.
> right: It isn't your ward
  mood -tiny
  say: You tell yourself she's only late. On your way out, the patient in the far bed is still awake, watching the dark.

@event infirmary_sister_fevered
title: The Healer's Own Fever
thread: missed_round
trigger: queued
tone: tense
art: potion
scene: A small office behind a hospital ward, a grey-faced healer upright in her chair with a compress on her neck and a list of patients in her lap.
text: You find her in the little office behind the ward, upright in her chair, a cold compress on her own neck and the patient list in her lap. Her face is grey. "Lake Fever," she says. "From bed six. It's nothing. Go to bed."
> left: Fetch the Headmistress
  alignment +tiny
  rel amaranth -small
  flag rounds_reported
  queue infirmary_covering_rounds in 1
  say: The headmistress comes in her dressing gown, and two healers from the village by morning. Sister Amaranth doesn't speak to you for a week. Then she thanks you, as an instruction: "Next time, run faster."
> right: Do the rounds she dictates
  health -small
  rel amaranth +small
  flag rounds_secret
  queue infirmary_covering_rounds in 1
  say: "Bed two, the blue bottle, half a spoon. Bed four, turn the charm. Bed six…" She stops. "Bed six will lie to you. Don't believe her." You do the rounds until midnight.

@event infirmary_covering_rounds
title: Eight Beds, One of You
thread: missed_round
trigger: queued
slot: evening
tone: tense
when: not flag(rounds_covered)
art: cauldron
scene: A row of infirmary beds at evening, a student in rolled-up sleeves measuring a remedy while a healer gives orders from a chair.
text: {flag(rounds_secret): Sister Amaranth still can't stand, and is still giving orders from her chair.|The village healers are run off their feet, and Sister Amaranth, propped up in her chair, is giving them orders too.} Eight beds, eight remedies, one of you. A second-year with a burned hand wants to know if it will scar.
> left: Do every bed properly
  study -tiny
  challenge brew apprentice+:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      rel amaranth +small
      flag rounds_covered
      say: Every bed, every spoon, every charm on the hour. The burned hand won't scar. At midnight the chair says, "Bed three. Well done," and then you hear her fall asleep sitting up.
    lose:
      health -tiny
      flag rounds_covered
      say: You mix up beds three and five. Nobody notices but her. She makes you recite every dose on the ward back to her, with your eyes shut, before she lets you sleep.
  queue infirmary_bed_six now
> right: Do the urgent ones, then revise
  rel amaranth -tiny
  flag rounds_covered
  queue infirmary_bed_six now
  say: You see to the burns and the fevers and leave the rest for the morning. From the chair comes a small, disappointed silence.

@event infirmary_bed_six
title: Bed Six's Story
thread: missed_round
trigger: queued
slot: evening
tone: tense
when: flag(rounds_covered) and not flag(bed_six_done)
art: potion
scene: A shivering second-year in an infirmary bed with blue-tinged ears, insisting on something, while a healer watches sceptically from a chair across the ward.
text: Bed six is a second-year with Lake Fever, blue to the ears, who swears she never went near the lake. "I fell asleep in the bath," she says. From her chair, Sister Amaranth raises one eyebrow and keeps it there. The fever isn't breaking. A cure for a bath is not a cure for the lake.
> left: Get the true story out of her
  challenge appeal apprentice:
    win:
      charm +small
      flag bed_six_truth
      say: It comes out in a rush: the flat rock, a dare, four of them. The other three are in her dormitory, coughing into their scarves so nobody will know. You fetch them down one by one. By midnight the ward is full, and all four fevers are breaking.
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -small
      say: She sticks to the bath. You treat her for the lake anyway, on Sister Amaranth's say-so, and on Thursday three more second-years turn up blue to the ears.
  flag bed_six_done
  queue infirmary_own_prescription in 2
> right: Treat what she tells you
  health +tiny
  rel amaranth -small
  flag bed_six_done
  queue infirmary_own_prescription in 2
  say: You dose her for a chill and get an early night. The fever takes three days longer than it should, and the chair across the ward watches every one of them.

@event infirmary_own_prescription
title: Her Own Prescription
thread: missed_round
trigger: queued
slot: evening
class: milestone
tone: warm
when: flag(rounds_covered) and flag(bed_six_done)
art: potion
scene: A grey-faced healer in her apron holding a tray of tonics at an infirmary bedside, swaying slightly, while a student reaches to take the tray.
text: Sister Amaranth is up a week too soon, grey at the edges, taking the tray out for the evening round as if nothing had happened. At bed two she sways and puts a hand on the rail. {flag(bed_six_truth): Four second-years sit up in their beds to watch.|The whole ward goes quiet.} Everyone looks at you.
> left: "Bed."
  health -small
  rel amaranth +medium
  flag rounds_finished
  say: You take the tray out of her hands and say it the way she says it to everyone else. She looks at you for a long moment. Then she goes to bed. You do the round. In the morning there's a card on the tray, in her square hand: "Bed four needs less."
> right: Walk the round beside her
  mood -tiny
  rel amaranth +small
  alchemy +small
  flag rounds_finished
  say: You don't argue. You carry the tray and have each bottle ready before she reaches for it. At the last bed she sits down on the end of it, and lets you measure out her own dose. "Half a spoon," she says. "I'm not a horse."

# ---------------- Red ink ----------------

@thread red_ink
from: story
with: begun=flag(red_ink_begun)
class: story
repeat: 1
tags: study
clues: lead_red_ink
members:
  start lecture_tower_red_ink

@event lecture_tower_red_ink
title: Two Sets of Marks
place: lecture_tower
time: morning afternoon
tone: light
when: year >= 2 and not flag(red_ink_begun)
repeat: 3
weight: 14
art: scroll
scene: A student on a spiral stair reading an essay covered in neat pencil and, beneath it, furious corrections in red ink.
text: Your returned essay has two sets of marks. The marker's, in polite pencil: "Good." And another, in red ink and unsigned, in a small round hand, savaging every paragraph and fixing three of them. The last line reads: "You can do better than good. Paragraph four proves it."
> left: Rewrite it properly
  mood -tiny
  lore +medium
  study +tiny
  flag red_ink_begun
  find lead_red_ink
  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_reply in 1
  say: You rewrite it twice. On the third morning you leave it on the second-floor stair, where you found the first. By evening it's gone.
> right: Bin it
  mood +tiny
  say: Good is good. You drop the red pages in the scorched-room bin, and feel a little foolish about how much you want to fish them out.

@event lecture_tower_red_ink_reply
title: A Question in the Margin
thread: red_ink
trigger: queued
tone: light
when: not flag(red_ink_answered) and not flag(red_ink_glimpse)
art: paper
scene: An essay left on a stone stair, its pages bleeding red ink, a single question written small at the bottom of the last page.
text: The essay is back on the stair, bleeding red. Most of it is about your argument. At the bottom, smaller: "Why do you want to be good at this?" It's the first question the ink has asked.
> left: Answer it honestly in the margin
  lore +tiny
  mood -tiny
  flag red_ink_answered
  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_front_row in 1
  say: You write three lines you'd never say aloud, and leave the essay on the stair. It's gone by noon. You spend the afternoon wishing you'd written two.
> right: Wait on the stair to see who takes it
  challenge deceive master- likely:
    win:
      guile +small
      say: You hide on the landing above. A first-year in robes two sizes too big gathers up the pages, reads the first line right there on the step, and makes a small, furious noise.
    lose:
      health -tiny
      say: You fall asleep on the stair. When you wake the essay is gone, and there's a note on your chest in red: "Paragraph six. Also, you snore."
  flag red_ink_glimpse
  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_front_row in 1

@event lecture_tower_red_ink_front_row
title: The Front Row
thread: red_ink
trigger: queued
slot: morning
tone: warm
when: (flag(red_ink_answered) or flag(red_ink_glimpse)) and not flag(red_ink_met)
art: book
scene: A lecture hall of first-years, one small student alone in the front row with red ink on her fingers, looking down at a page of perfect notes while the lecturer waits.
text: You cut through the first-years' lecture on your way somewhere else. In the front row, alone, sits a first-year with red ink on her fingers. The lecturer asks her a question. She opens her mouth, and nothing comes out, and the hall waits until she looks at the floor. {flag(red_ink_glimpse): The robes are the ones from the stair, two sizes too big.|Her notes, you can see from the aisle, are perfect.}
> left: Sit by her afterwards, and write
  study -tiny
  lore +small
  flag red_ink_met
  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_seminar in 1
  say: You sit down beside her after the hall empties and slide your last essay across, without a word. She writes on it for twenty minutes without looking up. At the bottom: "Seminar on Thursday. I have to speak. I can't."
> right: Leave her the secret
  lore +tiny
  mood +tiny
  flag red_ink_met
  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_seminar in 1
  say: You walk on, and leave your next essay on the stair as usual. It comes back with the usual red, and one line more at the bottom: "Seminar on Thursday. I have to speak. Please don't come."

@event lecture_tower_red_ink_seminar
title: Thursday's Seminar
thread: red_ink
trigger: queued
slot: afternoon
class: milestone
tone: warm
when: flag(red_ink_met)
art: scroll
scene: A small seminar room, a first-year frozen at a lectern holding her paper in both hands, a dozen students waiting, one older student at the back half out of their seat.
text: Thursday. The first-year stands at the seminar lectern with her paper in both hands and no voice at all. A dozen students wait. The lecturer looks at the clock. Her eyes find you at the back of the room.
> left: Stand up and argue with her paper
  challenge research apprentice+:
    win:
      lore +small
      renown +small
      say: You stand and say her fourth paragraph is wrong. It isn't, and she knows it, and she's so annoyed she forgets to be frightened. She takes your objection apart for ten minutes. The room applauds, mostly for her.
    lose:
      mood -small
      lore +tiny
      say: You stand and object, and your objection is bad, and she's too polite to say so out loud. She sits down without reading. Your essay comes back that week with one line of red: "Good try. Bad argument."
  flag red_ink_finished
> right: Offer to read it for her
  mood +small
  renown -tiny
  flag red_ink_finished
  say: You walk to the front and hold out your hand. She shakes her head, then gives you the first page. You read the first paragraph aloud. She takes the second page back, and reads the rest herself, quietly, all the way to the end.

# ---------------- The late bloomer ----------------

@thread late_bloomer
from: story
with: begun=flag(bloomer_begun)
class: story
repeat: 1
tags: magic
clues: lead_late_bloomer
members:
  start home_market_late_bloomer

@event home_market_late_bloomer
title: The Cold Stove
place: home_market
tone: warm
when: not flag(bloomer_begun)
repeat: 4
weight: 16
art: cauldron
scene: The back of a small sweet shop, a copper sugar pan boiling over into dark caramel on a stove with no fire in it, a young woman staring at her own hands.
text: The sweet shop is the one thing in town that hasn't changed. Then the owner's daughter, a year or two older than you, bangs down a ladle and swears, and the sugar pan on the cold stove boils over into caramel. There is no fire under it. Her father looks at you, then at your castle robes. "You're the one who'd know," he says quietly.
> left: Show her how to breathe it down
  mana -small
  arcana +small
  flag bloomer_begun
  find lead_late_bloomer
  say: You show her the first thing anyone at the castle learns: breathe in for four, hold the heat, let it go for four. The pan stops seething. She looks at her hands as if they belonged to someone else.
> right: "It happens. It'll settle."
  mood -tiny
  say: Her father nods, glad to be told so. Behind him she scrapes the burned sugar out of the pan, very carefully, and doesn't go near the stove again.

@event home_fields_bloomer_lessons
title: Where Nothing Can Catch
thread: late_bloomer
place: home_fields
tone: light
when: not flag(bloomer_steady)
weight: 16
art: flame
scene: A young woman and a student in castle robes on a bare, stony patch past the last field row at sunset, the young woman holding a candle stub at arm's length, its flame far too tall.
text: She's waiting past the last of the long rows, where the ground is all stones and nothing can catch, with a candle stub in each pocket. "Teach me something," she says. "Properly. Before you go back."
> left: Teach her to keep a flame small
  challenge spell apprentice+:
    win:
      arcana +small
      mood +small
      flag bloomer_steady
      queue home_cottage_bloomer_father in 1
      say: By sunset she can hold a flame the size of a fingernail for ten breaths. She counts every one of them out loud.
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: The candle goes off like a firework. You both lie in the grass laughing until the farmer shouts. "Next time," she says.
> right: Spend the afternoon at home
  health +small
  say: You promise her next time. She shrugs, the way people do when they've stopped expecting things.

@event home_cottage_bloomer_father
title: Sweets on the Step
thread: late_bloomer
trigger: queued
tone: warm
when: flag(bloomer_steady) and not flag(bloomer_father_seen)
defer: not has(plan_home)
art: door
scene: A worried shopkeeper at a cottage door holding a small paper bag of sweets, not stepping inside.
text: Her father is at the door with a paper bag of the sweets you liked when you were small. He doesn't come in. "They take them young, at the castle, don't they," he says. "She's past it. So what is it for, all that fire, in a sweet shop?"
> left: Tell him the truth
  mood -small
  alignment +tiny
  flag bloomer_father_seen
  queue home_market_bloomer_charm in 1
  say: "I don't know what it's for," you tell him. "I don't think anyone does, at her age." He thanks you for not lying, and leaves the sweets on the step.
> right: Promise to ask at the castle
  mood +small
  flag bloomer_father_seen
  flag bloomer_promise
  queue home_market_bloomer_charm in 1
  say: His face does something complicated. "Would you," he says, not quite a question. You hear yourself promise. The sweets taste exactly as they used to.

@event home_market_bloomer_charm
title: A Lid on the Pot
thread: late_bloomer
trigger: queued
slot: afternoon
tone: tense
when: flag(bloomer_father_seen) and not flag(bloomer_charm_done)
defer: not has(plan_home)
art: gem
scene: A market charm seller holding up a grey glass bead on a cord to a young woman counting coins from her apron.
text: In the market you find her at the charm seller's stall. He's holding up a grey glass bead on a cord. "Lumenhold-blessed," he's saying. "Smothers it, like a lid on a pot. No more flares, no more fuss." It's plain glass; you can see that from here. She's counting out her savings.
> left: Let her believe in it
  mood -small
  flag bloomer_damped
  flag bloomer_charm_done
  queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1
  say: You say nothing. She buys it and puts it on. It's only glass, but she believes in it, and the sugar pans stay cold all week. She looks lighter, and smaller.
> right: Tell her it's only glass
  challenge appeal apprentice+:
    win:
      charm +small
      queue home_market_bloomer_ice now
      say: "It's glass," you tell her. "You did the hard part yourself, out past the fields." The charm seller gives you the look of a man revising his prices. She puts her coins back in her apron, slowly.
    lose:
      mood -small
      flag bloomer_damped
      queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1
      say: She buys it anyway. "Easy for you," she says, not unkindly. "You get to go back."
  flag bloomer_charm_done

@event home_market_bloomer_ice
title: Ice in the Square
thread: late_bloomer
trigger: queued
class: incident
tone: tense
when: flag(bloomer_charm_done) and not flag(bloomer_damped) and not flag(bloomer_ice_done)
art: snow
scene: A market-square horse trough frozen solid around a carter's arm, a panicking young woman beside it and frost spreading across the cobbles.
text: Not ten minutes later, a shout from the square. The horse trough has frozen solid, with a carter's arm in it to the elbow, and she's standing beside it white with panic. Frost is creeping across the cobbles from her feet.
> left: Break the ice by hand
  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:
    win:
      health -small
      vigor +small
      say: You hammer at the ice with a cobble until it gives with a crack like a door. The carter pulls his arm free, swearing, mostly at the trough.
    lose:
      health -medium
      say: You get him free in the end, but the ice takes the skin off your knuckles doing it. You'll feel that trough for days.
  flag bloomer_ice_done
  queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1
> right: Talk her down so it melts
  challenge appeal adept-:
    win:
      charm +small
      mood +small
      say: "Four in. Hold. Four out." Her breath slows, and the ice goes grey, then soft, then water. The carter stares at his arm, and then at her.
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -small
      say: It takes an hour, three kettles from the inn and a carter who will tell this story for years.
  flag bloomer_ice_done
  queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1

@event home_cottage_bloomer_letter
title: A Flame That Stays
thread: late_bloomer
trigger: queued
slot: evening
defer: not has(plan_home)
class: milestone
tone: warm
when: flag(bloomer_charm_done) and (flag(bloomer_ice_done) or flag(bloomer_damped)) and not flag(bloomer_finished)
art: candle
scene: A young woman at a cottage table at evening, a small steady candle flame burning on the tip of her finger.
text: {flag(bloomer_damped): She comes by one evening, the grey bead on its cord. "I know it's only glass," she says. "I keep it in my pocket anyway." She lights a candle with her finger, small and steady, to show you.|She comes by one evening with a candle she lit at noon and has kept burning, small and steady, all day. "Look," she says. "It stays."}
> left: Write to the castle for her
  gold -tiny
  mood +medium
  flag bloomer_letter
  flag bloomer_finished
  say: {flag(bloomer_promise): You keep your promise to her father and write to the castle that night|You write to the castle that night}: what she can do, what she learned with no teacher but you, and that some people come late to things. You pay the postage; she walks it to the post office herself.
> right: Leave her your practice candle
  study -tiny
  mood +medium
  flag bloomer_finished
  say: {flag(bloomer_promise): You never did ask at the castle. Instead you leave her|You leave her} the practice candle you've had since your first week, the one you learned on, burned down to a stub. A month later a letter comes with a drawing of the stub, still lit.
